 ## Overview
-Marching Squares is a 2D contouring algorithm that extracts isocontour line segments or polygonal meshes from a discrete 2D scalar field by processing each grid cell independently [S1, S2, unofficial]. It determines the topology of the isosurface intersection within a square cell based on the scalar values at the four corners relative to an isolevel [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike its 3D counterpart, Marching Cubes, which can suffer from ambiguous topologies in specific cases, the 2D version has no ambiguous cases for a single isolevel, ensuring topological correctness for standard scalar fields [S1, S2, unofficial]. It serves as the foundational 2D case for the [Marching Cubes](774-marching-cubes.md) algorithm.
