+2.  **Evaluate corners:** Retrieve the scalar values at the four corners of the current cell in the order: bottom-left $(x, y)$, bottom-right $(x+1, y)$, top-right $(x+1, y+1)$, and top-left $(x, y+1)$ [S1, S2, unofficial]. Let these be $v_0, v_1, v_2, v_3$.
+3.  **Compute case index:** Calculate an integer case index $C \in [0, 15]$ using binary encoding based on which corners are inside the isosurface ($v_i \ge \text{IsoValue}$) [S2, unofficial]:
+    $$ C = \sum_{i=0}^{3} b_i 2^i, \quad \text{where } b_i = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } v_i \ge \text{IsoValue} \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} $$
+4.  **Handle trivial cases:** If $C = 0$ (all corners below isovalue) or $C = 15$ (all corners above isovalue), no intersection occurs; continue to the next cell [S2, unofficial].
+5.  **Determine intersections:** For each of the four edges of the square, check if the isovalue lies between the corner values. An edge between corners $i$ and $j$ is intersected if $(v_i \ge \text{IsoValue}) \neq (v_j \ge \text{IsoValue})$ [S1, unofficial].
+6.  **Interpolate intersection points:** For each intersected edge between corners $i$ and $j$ with values $v_i, v_j$ and spatial coordinates $P_i, P_j$, calculate the exact intersection point $P$ using linear interpolation [G]:
     $$ P = P_i + \frac{\text{IsoValue} - v_i}{v_j - v_i} (P_j - P_i) $$
-    where $P_i$ and $P_j$ are the spatial coordinates of the corners [S1, unofficial].
-7.  **Connect Segments:** Connect the calculated intersection points to form the output geometry based on the case $C$:
-    *   **Single Corner Cases (1, 2, 4, 8):** One corner is inside. Connect the two adjacent intersected edges [S1, unofficial].
-    *   **Opposite Corner Cases (3, 5, 6, 9, 10, 12):** Two opposite corners are inside. Connect the two opposite intersected edges [S1, unofficial].
-    *   **Adjacent Corner Cases (7, 11, 13, 14):** Three corners are inside (equivalent to one outside). Connect the two adjacent intersected edges of the *outside* corner [S1, unofficial].
-    *   *Note on Orientation:* To ensure consistent winding (e.g., clockwise around the solid), the direction of returned edges may need to be swapped if the case number is even [S1, unofficial].
+7.  **Connect segments:** Return line segments based on $C$ [S1, unofficial]:
+    *   Cases 1/14, 2/13, 4/11, 8/7: Return a single edge connecting the two adjacent intersected edges.
+    *   Cases 3/12, 5/10: Return a single edge connecting the two opposite intersected edges.
+    *   Case 9: Return two edges following the logic of cases 1 and 8.
+    *   Case 6: Return two edges following the logic of cases 2 and 4.
+    *   If $C$ is even, swap the direction of the returned edge(s) to ensure clockwise winding around the solid [S1, unofficial].
 
