 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_x N_y)$, as each cell is processed independently in constant time [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_x N_y)$ for the input grid. Output size is proportional to the number of intersected cells.
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, topologically correct for 2D scalar fields (no ambiguous cases like in 3D), fast [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Only applicable to 2D data. Approximates the isosurface with linear segments, which may not capture high-frequency details without fine grid resolution [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Extracting 2D contours from scalar fields (e.g., height maps, 2D simulations, slice extraction from 3D volumes).
-*   **When not to use:** For 3D volumes (use [Marching Cubes](774-marching-cubes.md)) or when high-fidelity smooth surfaces are required without post-processing (consider [Marching Tetrahedra](1733-marching-tetrahedra.md) for better normal estimation).
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_x N_y)$, as each cell is processed independently in constant time [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_x N_y)$ for the input grid; output size is proportional to the number of intersected cells [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, processes cells independently, and extracts 2D contours efficiently [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Approximates the isosurface with linear segments, which may not capture high-frequency details without fine grid resolution [S1, unofficial]. Requires a vertex buffer for mesh connectivity [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Extracting 2D contours from scalar fields (e.g., height maps, 2D simulations, slice extraction from 3D volumes) [G].
+*   **When not to use:** For 3D volumes (use [Marching Cubes](774-marching-cubes.md)) or when high-fidelity smooth surfaces are required without post-processing (consider [Marching Tetrahedra](1733-marching-tetrahedra.md) for better normal estimation) [G].
 
 ## Background
-Marching Squares is the 2D analog of the Marching Cubes algorithm. It was developed as a standard technique for contouring 2D scalar fields. The algorithm processes each square cell independently, determining the intersection of the isosurface with the cell edges based on the vertex values. It is widely used in image processing, geographic information systems (GIS) for contour lines, and as a component in 3D volume rendering pipelines for extracting 2D slices. The method is robust and does not suffer from the topological ambiguities found in 3D Marching Cubes cases 5 and 6 [S1, S2, unofficial].
